<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Komnenian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1081">1081</a>–<a href="/years/1185">1185</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Komnenian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Komnenian dynasty ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from 1081 to 1185, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>. Its emperors restored Byzantine military and political strength after the crises of the eleventh century, partly by relying on family networks and Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. After the dynasty's fall in 1185 the empire weakened rapidly, a decline that culminated in the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Komnenos family was Byzantine Greek. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-psellos">Michael Psellos</a> reported that it came from the village of Komne in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, a view commonly accepted by modern scholars. The first known member, Manuel Erotikos Komnenos, acquired large estates at Kastamon in Paphlagonia, which became the family stronghold. The family thus became associated with the powerful military aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, so that although it came from Thrace it was considered eastern. Isaac I Komnenos, his son, led a coup against Michael VI in 1057 and reigned until 1059, when courtiers pressed him to abdicate and become a monk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The dynasty returned to the throne with Isaac's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-i-komnenos">Alexios I Komnenos</a>, who married Irene Doukaina, grandniece of Constantine X Doukas. The two clans were afterwards often called Komnenodoukai. Under Alexios and his successors the empire was fairly prosperous and stable. Alexios moved the imperial palace to the Blachernae quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> was recovered from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> crossed Byzantine territory, and the family intermarried with the ruling houses of the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>.</p><p>Alexios ruled for 37 years, his son John II for 25, and John's son Manuel I for another 37. Succession followed no fixed order, and after Manuel's reign the family fell into conspiracies. Alexios II ruled for three years, and his conqueror Andronikos I for two, before the Angelos family took the throne under Isaac II, who was dethroned and blinded by his own brother Alexios III. Andronikos executed many relatives who opposed him, and opposition to Isaac II also cost family members their lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Weeks before the crusaders took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, one branch fled to Paphlagonia and the Pontic Alps and founded the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>. Its rulers, the Grand Komnenoi, held it until 1461, when it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-komnenos">David Komnenos</a>, and his family were executed two years later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. Another branch, using the name Komnenos Doukas, founded the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in 1204. A renegade, Isaac Komnenos, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> from 1184 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> took the island in 1191.</p><p>The Palaiologoi, a closely related family, ruled the restored empire from 1261 until 1453. John Komnenos Molyvdos, a scholar and physician who died in 1719, is often considered the last descendant. Many later claims of Komnenian descent are almost certainly fiction.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Komnenos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Komnenian dynasty</b> (1081–1185) recovered much ground through military reorganisation and diplomatic skill, exploiting the First Crusade to regain western Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>With the support of the capital's populace, the plotters seized most of the Great Palace, and John Komnenos, a descendant of the <b>Komnenian dynasty</b>, was crowned in Hagia Sophia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-31">31 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He was the first reigning member of the <b>Komnenian dynasty</b> and ruled until 1059.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
